from ../plugins import Plugins

export anchor Migration:
    description:
        The migrate plugin, bundled with the compiler, notices projects written
        for an older Piton and offers to bring them up to date. It is a plugin,
        described in @{Plugins}, that inspects every project before a command
        runs, and adds `piton migrate`.

        Its source is in the compiler's repository at plugins/migrate: its
        declaration in plugin.pi and its code in src. It has no Piton files of
        its own.

    migrations:
        - @{FrameworksToPlugins}
        - @{VendoredKeywords}
    command: @{MigrateCommand}

    capabilities:
        It may read files in the project, and propose edits to piton.config.pi
        and the project's .pi files, which are the only files a migration
        changes. It runs no programs and writes nothing itself.

    adding:
        A migration recognises one old way of writing a project, from the
        project's files as they are, without compiling them, and gives the whole
        new contents of each file it changes. When Piton changes in a way that
        breaks existing projects, the change comes with a migration.

export anchor FrameworksToPlugins:
    description:
        A configuration that lists its frameworks under frameworks, which is now
        an error, lists them under plugins instead: a framework is a plugin.

    detects:
        A key named frameworks in the piton-config anchor of piton.config.pi.

    edit:
        When the configuration has no plugins key, the frameworks key is renamed
        plugins, and its items stay as they are. When it has both, the
        frameworks items move to the end of the plugins list, and the frameworks
        key goes. Either key can hold its items as a block or as an inline list;
        an inline plugins list becomes a block, so the moved items can follow
        its own. Every configuration that has a frameworks key gets this edit,
        so none is left to change by hand.

export anchor VendoredKeywords:
    description:
        A skill or an agent that a vendored file supplies whole, declared with
        skill or agent, which no longer compiles, is declared with
        vendored-skill or vendored-agent instead. The keyword decides which
        properties the compiler requires: a skill needs useWhen and an agent
        needs role, and their vendored forms need neither.

    detects:
        In each .pi file below the source root, a declaration that starts with
        skill or agent at the start of a line, with or without export. A skill
        is vendored when a folder that its files names, inline or for any
        target, holds a SKILL.md. An agent is vendored when it has a file that
        is not null. Files below a tethers directory are left out: an installed
        package is changed in its own project.

    edit:
        The keyword of each such declaration becomes vendored-skill or
        vendored-agent, and the rest of the line stays as it is. A skill whose
        folder has no SKILL.md stays a skill.

export anchor MigrateCommand:
    commandName: migrate

    description:
        Applies every migration the project needs, without asking, since running
        it is the asking, and reports each file changed. A project that needs
        none is said to be up to date.

    namedArguments:
        check:
            Change nothing, and fail when the project needs a migration, naming
            each one, for CI.

    exitCode:
        success: 0
        outOfDate: 1, with --check, when a migration is needed
